# Methodology

Here is exactly how the calculator works.

## How It Works

The calculator uses the "Classic 4% Rule" from William Bengen's 1994 research.

- Year 1: Withdraw 4% of your initial portfolio.
- Year 2+: Increase that dollar amount by each year's inflation.
- Withdrawals are annual (beginning of each year).
- The remaining portfolio grows/shrinks with S&P 500 returns.
- For comparison, the same withdrawals are also run on the Trinity study's (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trinity_study) stock/bond mixes (75/25, 50/50, 25/75, 0/100), rebalanced to the target mix every year.

Example: $1M portfolio → $40k Year 1 → if 3% inflation → $41,200 Year 2.

Order of operations each year: withdraw, then apply the year's portfolio return, then subtract the annual fee from the year-end balance. A start year fails if the balance hits zero before the horizon. Success rate = share of start years with at least horizon years of data that did not fail.

## Data Sources

- S&P 500 returns & CPI inflation: Robert Shiller, Yale University (https://shillerdata.com/). Includes monthly S&P 500 prices, dividends, and CPI from 1871 to present. We use annual returns (January to January). Dataset: https://img1.wsimg.com/blobby/go/e5e77e0b-59d1-44d9-ab25-4763ac982e53/downloads/ie_data.xls
- Bond returns: the same dataset's monthly total returns on 10-year U.S. Treasuries (GS10), compounded January to January. The original Trinity study used long-term high-grade corporate bonds, so its success rates differ slightly.
- Nasdaq Composite returns: DQYDJ's NASDAQ Annual Return Rankings (https://dqydj.com/nasdaq-annual-return-rankings/), calendar-year total return with dividends reinvested, 1972 onward, built from FRED's NASDAQCOM series. These run December to December while the Shiller S&P 500 series runs January to January, so the two are offset by one month. QQQ tracks the Nasdaq-100 (since 1985), a narrower index that behaves very similarly.

The raw yearly data is available as JSON at <%= request.base_url %>/api/v1/data.

## Fees and Taxes

Fees. Results assume no fees unless you pick an annual fee under Portfolio Parameters. The fee is charged on the portfolio at the end of each year, like a fund's expense ratio or an advisor's fee. It matters more than it looks: with $1M at 4% over 30 years, the historical success rate is <%= @fee_example[0] %>% with no fees and <%= @fee_example[1] %>% with a 1% annual fee.

Taxes. Results are before taxes, because what you owe depends on which account the money comes from:

- Taxable brokerage account: only the gain is taxed. Selling shares gives you back your cost basis tax-free, and the profit is taxed as a capital gain (0%, 15% or 20% federally for shares held over a year). Dividends are taxed every year, even when reinvested.
- Traditional 401(k) or IRA: every dollar withdrawn is taxed as ordinary income.
- Roth 401(k) or IRA: qualified withdrawals are tax-free.

The simplest way to account for taxes is to treat the withdrawal as your pre-tax budget. If you need $40,000 a year after tax and expect to pay about 10% in tax, plan to withdraw about $44,400 a year. This is general information, not tax advice.

## Assumptions

- 100% S&P 500, plus stock/bond mixes: The main results put all money in the S&P 500. The stock/bond comparison holds the rest in 10-year Treasuries and rebalances annually.
- Before taxes, fees optional: Results are before taxes. Fees are zero unless you pick an annual fee in the calculator.
- Annual withdrawals: Taken at the beginning of each year.
- Dividends reinvested: Returns include dividend reinvestment.
